## System Design Operating Protocol — v1.0

Here's the standing framework we operate from. Every idea you bring gets processed through this pipeline without needing to re-establish ground rules.

---

### Intake Protocol

When you bring an idea, I need these inputs before design begins:

| Input | What I Need | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Problem Domain** | What pain does this solve | Determines architecture class |
| **User Classes** | Who uses it, at what privilege level | Drives auth model + permission tiers |
| **Scale Target** | Expected load (users, requests/day) | Infrastructure sizing decisions |
| **Data Sensitivity** | PII? Financial? Health? | Security classification |
| **Platform Target** | Web / Mobile / API-only / Internal tool | Framework assignment |
| **Existing Systems** | What it integrates with | Integration surface design |

If you don't have all of these, I derive reasonable defaults and flag assumptions explicitly.

---

### Framework Assignment Rules (Non-Negotiable)

```
Content-driven + SEO-critical + public-facing       → Next.js App Router + PostgreSQL/Prisma
Enterprise + precision-critical + internal/B2B      → Angular + FastAPI + MongoDB
API-only / microservice                             → FastAPI standalone
AI/ML workload                                      → FastAPI Python microservice (sidecar or standalone)
```

---

### Design Pipeline — Fixed Order

```
Phase 0  →  Domain Modelling
             - Bounded contexts
             - Entity relationships
             - Data classification

Phase 1  →  System Architecture
             - Component diagram
             - Data flow
             - Integration surface
             - Framework assignment confirmed

Phase 2  →  Database Design
             - Schema / ERD
             - Normalization proof (if relational)
             - Index strategy

Phase 3  →  API Contract
             - OpenAPI spec (endpoints, request/response shapes)
             - Zod schemas derived from contract
             - Auth middleware mapping

Phase 4  →  Security Model
             - Auth strategy (NextAuth / JWT/HTTP-only)
             - Permission tiers (L1–L4)
             - L4 hard-block list

Phase 5  →  Infrastructure & Observability
             - Deployment targets (Vercel / Railway / Docker)
             - Monitoring hooks (Sentry, Better Stack, Prometheus)
             - CI/CD pipeline skeleton

Phase 6  →  Sequential Build Plan
             - Module list in dependency order
             - No module starts before its dependency is complete
```

I produce all six phases before any code is written. You can pause at any phase for review.

