## Extension-First Design — Standing Mandate

This applies to every system, every module, every decision. No exceptions.

---

### The Core Principle

```
Every component is built assuming:
  1. Its requirements will change
  2. A new consumer will need to use it
  3. A new variant will need to be added
  4. It will eventually be replaced without breaking what depends on it
```

---

### How This Manifests Per Layer

**Architecture**
```
- Modular monolith first, extraction paths defined upfront
- Bounded contexts respected — no cross-context direct DB reads
- Event-driven seams at context boundaries (even if sync today)
- Feature flags from day one — no hard-coded behavior switches
```

**Database**
```
- Schema versioned from migration 001 — never edited retroactively
- No application logic in the DB (no stored procedures as business rules)
- Nullable columns for future optional fields, not schema rewrites
- Lookup/reference tables for any value that could expand (statuses, types, roles)
```

**API**
```
- Versioned from v1 — /api/v1/* — even on day one
- Response envelopes always include metadata room: { data, meta, pagination }
- No breaking changes to existing fields — only additive changes
- Deprecation headers before removal, never silent drops
```

**Backend / Services**
```
- SOLID enforced — especially Open/Closed and Dependency Inversion
- Strategy pattern for anything that has variants (payment providers, notifiers, exporters)
- Repository pattern on all data access — swap DB without touching business logic
- Service interfaces defined before implementations
```

**Frontend**
```
- Component contracts over implementation — props as explicit interfaces
- No business logic in UI components — logic lives in hooks/services
- Design token system from day one — no hardcoded colors, spacing, or typography
- Slot/composition patterns over monolithic components
```

**Security & Auth**
```
- Permission system is data-driven, not code-driven
- Adding a new role = DB record, not a code deployment
- Scopes defined as constants, referenced by string — extensible without refactor
```

**Infrastructure**
```
- Environment config externalized — no env-specific code branches
- Service boundaries expose health + metrics endpoints from day one
- Docker-first — every service runs containerized regardless of current deployment target
```

---

### The Extension Checklist

Every module I design gets validated against this before it ships:

```
[ ] Can a new variant be added without modifying existing code?        (OCP)
[ ] Can the data layer be swapped without touching business logic?     (Repository)
[ ] Can a new consumer call this without needing internal knowledge?   (Encapsulation)
[ ] Can permissions be extended without a code deploy?                 (Data-driven auth)
[ ] Can this be extracted into its own service if load demands it?     (Modular boundary)
[ ] Are all config values externalized?                                (12-Factor)
[ ] Is the API contract additive-safe?                                 (API versioning)
```

If any box is unchecked, the design is revised before we proceed.

---

### Constitution Entry

```
[EXT-E01]  Every system is built for extension. Modification of existing 
           contracts to add features is a design failure.

[EXT-E02]  Variants are added via strategy/plugin patterns, not conditionals.

[EXT-E03]  Permission and role models are always data-driven.

[EXT-E04]  API contracts are versioned from v1, day one. Breaking changes 
           are prohibited — only additive evolution.

[EXT-E05]  No business logic is coupled to infrastructure. Swap-ability 
           is a first-class design constraint.
```

